ASH for JetBrains IDEs
======================

What this plugin is, and what it deliberately is not
----------------------------------------------------

It runs the `ash` command line tool over the open project and turns what ASH reports into
editor annotations. It contains no scanners, no copy of ASH, and no scanner content of any
kind. It invokes whatever `ash` is on the user's PATH, or the executable named in
Settings | Tools | ASH, and reads the SARIF that ASH already writes at
`<output-dir>/reports/ash.sarif`.

So there is nothing here to keep in step with an ASH release. That is the reason the plugin
version is not in pyproject.toml's [tool.commitizen] version_files, and build.gradle.kts
gives the full argument at the point where someone would add it.

Why this lives under editors/ and not under packaging/
------------------------------------------------------

Everything in packaging/ installs ASH, and every one of those packages bundles exactly one
wheel -- ASH's own -- which is the mechanical guard keeping them on the permitted side of
the publishing boundary described in packaging/README.md. This installs nothing and bundles
nothing. It is a consumer of ASH rather than a distribution of it, and it would be the only
directory under packaging/ with no wheel to count.

editors/<editor>/ also holds a second editor integration without either owning the other's
tree.

Zero runtime dependencies, and why that is a requirement rather than a preference
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A JetBrains plugin distribution zip bundles its RUNTIME classpath into `lib/`. A single
`implementation` dependency therefore puts a third party's jar inside an artifact this
project publishes as a release asset, silently, at package time. packaging/README.md draws
that line: ASH's own code may ship in a published artifact, third-party code never may.

So this plugin has no runtime dependencies at all. Two consequences worth knowing before
changing anything:

  * The SARIF reader carries its own JSON parser, written against nothing but the JDK
    (Json.java). The IntelliJ Platform does bundle JSON libraries in its own lib/, and a
    compileOnly dependency on one would also keep the zip clean; that was rejected because
    which libraries the platform ships is not part of the plugin SDK's compatibility
    contract. A plugin relying on it gets a NoClassDefFoundError on some future IDE, and for
    a security tool that renders as zero findings.
  * assert-plugin-zip-contents.py is the enforcement, and it is phrased as a list comparison
    for the same reason the one-wheel rule is phrased as a count: "no third-party jars" needs
    a decision per dependency, made by whoever reviewed the build file that day. "Every jar
    in lib/ is named ash-jetbrains*" is checkable by reading the log.

It also refuses loose .class files in the zip, and refuses a class outside this plugin's own
package inside our own jar. The .deb and .rpm can only gain third-party code by gaining a
.whl, so counting wheels is sufficient there. A plugin build could instead shade or shadow
third-party classes into our jar, which leaves the jar count at one and the boundary crossed
anyway -- the same extra check packaging/flatpak/build.sh needs for `.dist-info` directories.

Build-time dependencies are a different question and are not the concern: the IntelliJ
Platform Gradle plugin, Gradle, JaCoCo, JUnit, and the roughly one gigabyte IDE distribution
resolved from JetBrains' CDN are all thrown away after the build.

The `ash` name collision on Windows
-----------------------------------

`ash` is canonical and is what this plugin invokes by default. It is also the name of the
Almquist shell, which MSYS2 ships on Windows and which has already shadowed ASH's entry
point in this project's CI, producing `Illegal option --`. JetBrains IDEs run on Windows and
this plugin spawns a process, so the collision reaches users here.

It cannot be left to fail naturally, because of the SHAPE of the failure. A shell that does
not understand `scan --source-dir ...` writes an error and exits, ASH writes no SARIF, and a
plugin that only knew how to read SARIF would put zero annotations on the file. Zero
annotations is what a clean file looks like. The wrong binary answering would therefore
render as "ASH found nothing", most confidently in the case where a real secret was present.

Three things address it:

  * Every scan is preceded by `ash --version`, and the plugin refuses to proceed unless the
    output contains `awslabs/automated-security-helper`. An exit code cannot distinguish ASH
    from any other program that answers `--version`, so the test is a string in ASH's own
    output. Measured: `ash --version` prints
    `awslabs/automated-security-helper v3.7.0` and exits 0.
  * The error names `automated-security-helper` as the fix. That entry point is kept
    indefinitely by the project for exactly this reason, so recommending it is not
    recommending something that may be removed. When the output looks like a shell rejecting
    an option, the message says so and names MSYS2.
  * The child's stdin is redirected from the null device. A shell invoked in a way it reads
    as interactive READS STDIN: with stdin inherited from the IDE, that child would never
    exit and never write anything, and the annotator would wait on a process that is waiting
    on it. The 30-second probe deadline bounds anything that ignores EOF.

`--version` and not `-v`: `-v` is `--verbose` in ASH and has been for all of v3, so a probe
using it would start a verbose scan instead of printing a version. `ashv3` still works and
warns once on stderr; it is deprecated, so nothing here suggests it, and AshExecutable
recognizes it only so a user who typed it into the settings field can be told where the
stderr line came from.

Building and verifying
----------------------

There is no JDK requirement to state beyond 21, and there is no need to install one: the
whole verification runs in a container.

  docker run --rm -v "$PWD":/work -w /work/editors/jetbrains gradle:jdk21 \
      ./verify-in-container.sh

That is the same script .github/workflows/ash-jetbrains-ci.yml runs. A verification that
exists twice drifts, and the copy in CI is the one nobody runs by hand, which is why the
steps are in the script rather than in the workflow YAML.

The image is used for its toolset -- a JDK 21, git, python3 -- and not for its Gradle: the
build always goes through `./gradlew`, which pins the Gradle version. That pin is
load-bearing. The wrapper was first generated at 8.14.3 and the IntelliJ Platform Gradle
plugin refused to apply, with "IntelliJ Platform Gradle Plugin requires Gradle Gradle 9.0.0
and higher". Gradle 9 also turns the configuration cache on by default, which is why
gradle.properties turns it back off.

Expect the first build to be slow. The IntelliJ Platform Gradle plugin resolves an IDE
distribution of roughly a gigabyte from JetBrains' CDN before it can compile anything.

The gates, and where each one lives
-----------------------------------

All four are Gradle tasks, so `./gradlew check buildPlugin` gates on a developer's machine
exactly as CI does, and each has one place to relax rather than two.

  assertTestsRan          Every compiled test suite ran and reported, none was skipped, and
                          AnnotationCountTest is among them.
  assertCoverage          Line and branch coverage, the denominator floors, and the exclusion
                          list audit.
  assertDistributionContents
                          Every jar in the built zip was built here, no loose class files, and
                          no foreign class inside our own jar.
  verifyPluginProjectConfiguration and verifyPluginStructure
                          The platform's own two checks, provided by the IntelliJ Platform
                          Gradle plugin and not wired into `check` by it. They are the only
                          things that read META-INF/plugin.xml the way the platform will, so
                          nothing else here would notice a misspelled extension point or a
                          since-build the platform rejects. The full verifyPlugin task -- the
                          Plugin Verifier's compatibility run, which downloads an additional
                          IDE per target -- is deliberately not wired in.
  unitTest's own doLast   A zero test count, reported at the moment of the run.

The last of those is not the gate, and the distinction was measured. With the test sources
moved aside, Gradle reported "Task :unitTest NO-SOURCE" and BUILD SUCCESSFUL: a task Gradle
skips as NO-SOURCE runs none of its own actions, so the in-task guard was never reached in the
one case it existed for. assertTestsRan is a separate task that reads artifacts, so it fires
whether or not the test task ran, and it also catches a committed include filter running a
subset -- which reports green in every summary line.

Coverage, and why the number is not the whole gate
--------------------------------------------------

The coverage standard this repository holds itself to names Python and TypeScript, each
gated independently at 90% and never blended. This plugin is a third language and the
standard did not mention it. assert-coverage.py applies the same 90% here, to line and
branch separately, and prints it as this tree's own number. Nothing about it is combined
with anyone else's figure.

The header comment of .github/workflows/ash-typescript-ci.yml explains why a bare percentage
is insufficient: a threshold can be satisfied by measuring less, and the shrink looks like a
config tidy-up in review. The mechanism here differs from that workflow's because the tool
differs, and copying the mechanism rather than the reasoning would have been worse than not
checking. jest omits source no test imports from its report ENTIRELY, so an unmeasured file
is invisible; that is the hole assert-coverage-completeness.mjs closes. JaCoCo enumerates
compiled classes, so an untouched class appears at 0% and is visibly bad.

The cheap way to game a JaCoCo gate is therefore not a narrowed crawler root but a widened
exclusion pattern -- one line in coverage-exclusions.json, and the percentage rises with no
test written. So that file is what the gate audits hardest: every excluded class must be
named there with a reason, must still be tracked, must still match a class in the report,
and must be under a declared line budget that is re-measured on every run. The budget is
what stops an exclusion becoming a place to hide logic.

Three classes are excluded, all of them IntelliJ Platform glue whose overrides need a
PsiFile, an Editor, an AnnotationHolder or the platform's ApplicationManager. Each one is
written so that every decision it could have made lives in a measured class instead; the
budget is the mechanical check on that sentence.

Known limitations
-----------------

A scan is a whole-project scan, not a single-file scan, because several of ASH's scanners are
about relationships between files. On a large repository that is not something to run on
every keystroke, and the annotator only proceeds for a file that is on disk.

ASH's output goes to a directory under the JVM's temporary directory, named from a digest of
the project's absolute path, and never into the project. ASH's own default is
`.ash/ash_output` under the source directory, which is right for a command line and wrong
here: the IDE indexes the project tree, so a scan's reports would become source the next scan
reads and reports on, plus a burst of file-watcher events on every run. AshScanPlan refuses
an output directory inside the source directory rather than trusting the caller.

Findings with no line-bearing physical location are not shown. Some ASH scanners report about
a repository or a dependency graph rather than about a place in a file, and an annotation is a
range in a document. They are counted rather than discarded silently, so a run that shows
nothing can be told apart from a clean file.

A finding on a line the document no longer has is dropped rather than clamped. The user edits
after the scan, and clamping to the last line would move a security warning onto unrelated
code.

Nothing here publishes
----------------------

There is no publishPlugin task, no token, no Marketplace submission, and no registry of any
kind. The build resolves an IDE, compiles against it, and produces a zip under
build/distributions. Getting that zip to users is a separate decision that has not been made.
